<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neubrandenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neubrandenburg is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on Lake Tollense. It was founded in 1248 by the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and passed to Mecklenburg around 1300 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lordship-of-stargard">Lordship of Stargard</a>. Its medieval wall with four brick Gothic gates, among them the Treptow Gate and the Stargard Gate, is one of the best preserved in Germany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neubrandenburg.jpg" alt="Neubrandenburg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Botaurus-stellaris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tollensesee-02-06-2008-187.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Neubrandenburg stands on the shore of the lake Tollensesee in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a> and forms the urban centre of the Mecklenburg Lakeland. It is part of the European Route of Brick Gothic, which leads through seven countries along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> coast, and is nicknamed the City of Four Gates, Stadt der Vier Tore, for its medieval gates. Its Low German name is Niegenbramborg, meaning New Brandenburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region was left largely empty during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a> and was resettled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, who formed the Veleti, from the 7th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> subdued the area in the mid-12th century, German colonisation intensified after 1200. Premonstratensian monks founded Broda Abbey on the lakeshore about 1240. The city was founded in 1248, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a> decided to build a settlement in the northern part of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> and named it after the older city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> to the south. The city later became part of Mecklenburg.</p><p>It flourished as a trade centre until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. Swedish troops garrisoned it during the advance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a>, but forces of the Imperial Catholic League retook it in 1631 and were widely reported to have killed many Swedish and Scottish soldiers as they surrendered. From 1701 to 1934 Neubrandenburg belonged to Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>. The writer Fritz Reuter lived there from 1856 to 1863, and a railway joined it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1864.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> two prisoner-of-war camps, Stalag II-A and Oflag II-E/67, stood at Fünfeichen within the city limits. A few days before the war ended in 1945, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> burned down about 80 per cent of the old town, including the City Hall, the Grand Ducal Palace and St Mary's Church. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> the centre was rebuilt in the 1950s and 1960s, and in 1952 the city became the centre of a Bezirk. Its population rose from about 20,000 at the war's end to about 90,000 in the late 1980s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Neubrandenburg has kept its medieval city wall entire: it is 7 metres high with a perimeter of 2.3 kilometres and four Brick Gothic gates from the 14th and 15th centuries, among them the Stargarder Tor. St Mary's Church, completed in 1298, was restored in 1975 and houses a concert hall opened in 2001. The city has been the capital of the Mecklenburgische Seenplatte district since 2011 and has a University of Applied Sciences, founded in 1991. Its population is about 65,000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neubrandenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John received the Lordship of Stargard in the east, with <b>Neubrandenburg</b> and Strelitz, which Mecklenburg had acquired from Brandenburg in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-stargard">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Stargard</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1952 it was divided into the districts of Rostock, Schwerin and <b>Neubrandenburg</b>; the state was re-established in 1990 as Mecklenburg-Vorpommern.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-mecklenburg">Free State of Mecklenburg</a></li></ul></section>
